From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ram: a write port's take, per eight bits
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With more than one write port, each bit of a word has a data mux over
the ports, and each port's take, its data's select, was one net across
the whole word: a 32-bit word's take drove 32 data muxes, a 128-bit
word's 128. With the word's write enable and hold buffered per eight
bits, this was the worst path left on a two-write-port register file:
repair_design chained buffers on it and the data mux took the slew.

Each group of eight bits now takes a buffered copy of each port's take,
in the group's first bit's cell, as the word's write enable already
does; no net in a word drives more than a group's pins.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires with the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
